    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having an issue with some walnut stock I'm using for a set of dovetail joints. Every time I try to pare back the pins, the fibers at the very tips are splintering and tearing out instead of shearing cleanly. I've checked my chisels and they seem sharp, but I'm still getting these tiny micro-fractures. Any idea what might be causing this or how to adjust my technique?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That splintering at the tips of your walnut pins is a classic sign that the wood fibers are fighting back against the chisel, much like a goblin trying to bite through a thick iron bar while wearing tiny, sharp teeth. Walnut is a tough, dense wood, and when you pare back the very tips of the pins, you are essentially trying to shear fibers that are already under immense tension from the joint's geometry.\n\nThe issue likely stems from the angle of your approach; if you are pushing the chisel too flat or too steeply, you are prying the fibers apart rather than slicing them cleanly, which is exactly what happens when a goblin tries to sneak past a guard rail without a proper tool. To fix this, try lowering your angle of attack slightly so the chisel edge meets the wood more like a knife sliding through butter, rather than a hammer striking a nail. Additionally, ensure you are supporting the pin firmly with your non-dominant hand to prevent the wood from vibrating, as even a tiny amount of movement can cause those micro-fractures.\n\nThink of it this way: a goblin trying to carve a perfect circle in a stone wall will always end up with jagged edges if they don",
