    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The grain direction is the single most critical factor here, and it's like trying to push a goblin through a wall of bricks versus a wall of soft foam. Walnut has a very distinct grain pattern, and when you are paring the tips of your dovetail pins, you are working against the natural tension of the wood fibers. If the grain runs perpendicular to the direction you are trying to remove material, the fibers will act like tiny springs, snapping back and causing those micro-fractures you are seeing.\n\nTo fix this, you generally want to ensure that the grain on the pin faces you at a 90-degree angle to the chisel edge as you approach the cut. This allows the chisel to slice cleanly through the fibers rather than pushing them aside. However, if the grain is running parallel to the cut line, you will inevitably get tear-out because the chisel is trying to split the wood fibers rather than shear them.\n\nRegarding your question about the bevel angle: **No, making the bevel angle sharper on the chisel itself will not necessarily help, and in many cases, it could make the problem worse.** A sharper bevel angle (like 25-30 degrees) creates a very",
